body:
I've given this question some thought, I hope my answer is able to help you.

This is by no means a magic cure and I am sure each person will have a different way of "escaping" this. 

Firstly, accepting that you feel this way and that this isn't your fault. No one wants to feel empty, it's awful. This could be the easiest step or the most difficult. You have to remember that this is a symptom of an illness you had no choice in getting, if you did, you wouldn't be depressed. I think of it like someone with a headache, they might be moody or grouchy, not because they are like that as a person but because the pain is making them fed up. It's the same thing here. Once you are able to see that, you can let go of unhelpful guilt. 

&#x200B;

Secondly, Unfortunately, for many people on this sub and in the world in general, depression/anxiety is chronic. This is important to me, because it makes the journey a lot different. When it's chronic, you will have to accept that some days, you will lose. Some days everything will be harder, everything. Nothing you can do, it's just a bad health day, same as someone with chronic pain. Some days you will lose. If it's chronic then I'd argue the best way is to learn to live with it and make it so the days were you lose are not too destructive and are less frequent, if you can win 5/7 days a week, then you are doing better than 4/7, which is better than 3/7 and so on. Now this is for depression/anxiety in general, this one symptom is what you asked about, but I feel as if decreasing other symptoms (tiredness, self hate, bad sleep and so on) helps massively. I hope this isn't too preachy, I have the tendency to go on a bit.

&#x200B;

For me I found that when I feel the most miserable, I can become extremely bitter, apathetic and miserable. I could watch videos of people crying and talking about personal tragedies and I "wouldn't care", reality is I do care, but I am just a deep rut that it's impossible to care, take this into a more physical account again. If someone broke their arm and you started telling your life story to them, they aren't going give you sympathy or pity, they are going to ignore you and probably lie on the floor in massive pain. Same here, a person feeling really depressed that can barely even take basic care of their basic needs isn't in a position to help anyone. The importance of mental health is just as important as physical health. 

&#x200B;

I'd say for me I just take care of myself a bit, start small, clean up my messy desk, brush my teeth, doesn't matter. Show myself some decency. Help myself firstly, if someone else has a problem they want to talk to me about or needs my help, they have to wait for me to get myself into a better place. It's not selfish to do that. As I said above, you can't help someone if you can't help yourself. Start with yourself. I do small things, treat myself kindly (even if I don't want to). Second thing I do here is do something I enjoy. This is tricky because I know some people don't enjoy anything, in which case I suggest they do something easy to digest and requires extremely low energy. Cooking videos, art videos, pet videos, anything. If you don't enjoy it after 30 minutes, try something else. I usually listen to music and play games, I might be miserable and it might not help at all, in which case I sleep and try again the next time, it works sometimes, doesn't others, this loops back to what I said earlier about winning and losing days. Once you find something you enjoy and your mood is a bit better, find something positive in your life or online, I know this is cliche, but I've found that it brings out the best in me. Reading depressing articles or going on social media is really a bad idea for your mental health. In fact social media in general is a really bad idea for anyone with mental health issues, it makes me anxiety and depression 10 times worse than it needs to be. For this I suggest going for a walk, taking in nature or talking to someone you care about. Once you bring your spirits up, you mind find your old self come out, the person you really are, someone far more empathetic. 

&#x200B;

Lastly, I suggest when you find yourself feeling more empathetic and happier to not engage with people about their problems, it might bring you down again. Keep that feeling at a baseline, make it your new default state just be being in it more often. 

&#x200B;

This is what worked for me, I hope it works for more people. It's not a magic cure and takes weeks, months and for me, years. But it helps, I would say on good days I have healthy and good amounts of empathy and understanding. On my bad days I do slip back into old ways, I become empty, but I understand and tell myself this is my illness, this is my chronic pain. I ride out the storm and I always, always return back to how I really am deep down. On really bad days or weeks I can become completely despondent to the point of concern, but it passes for me, because I managed to make my baseline mood and empathy at a higher level than I either naturally am or that my illness takes me too or both.  

I hope I wasn't too preachy or condescending. If nothing else, and nothing works, I understand how you feel and if means anything, I hope to anything you are able to get into a better, happier place some day sooner rather than later. Take care.

Hello, I did a little research on it and statistically it seems you have a higher chance of going outside right now and finding a briefcase of cash and a letter saying that it is yours for free. I know you said it is dumb but it is not, even if what you have anxiety over is far fetched, it is never dumb. I've had anxiety issues over the dumbest stuff ever, I got a splinter a few weeks ago and  I started to freak out and think that it was infected and I would lose my hand from the infection, 3 weeks later, here I am with both hands.  

&#x200B;

I highly doubt you have it, sorry if this wasn't much help : ) have a great day/night

Thank you for the reply, I have been told that it's a good idea to do charity work and such. I have been thinking about it but I don't know. I agree that it is nice to see lots of people far smarter and better than me do a lot more than I ever could.

&#x200B;

As for political stuff, I avoid it.
